@duprez 
> I think that the calculated fields should not be part of the main entity and that is why I think that your solution is poorly focused. A calculated field is a field that appears circumstantially in a query, but does not belong to the entity. That's my opinion.

I guess there are two ways of looking at it: I am taking the perspective that while they are not core properties of the entity they still represent a field that is very much tied to that entity, even if only circumstantially.

The mapped property will need to be named and given a TS type regardless, at which point it essentially does belong to the entity (except with the optional/`?` modifier), so why not also give it its SQL data type immediately with an `@Computed` decorator?

The supplied expression in the entity would also be optional, and even if provided it would still need to be explicitly selected to be included (unless indicated otherwise by a config flag).

At least in my application most examples seem to be true to this description. They are mainly properties that vary depending on the logged in user, e.g. `selfVote: 1 | 0 | -1` on a `CommentEntity` which represents whether the user has upvoted or downvoted the comment. Ideally for me this would be defined as:

```ts
@Calculated("tinyint", {expression: "(SELECT vote FROM votes WHERE user.id = :userId AND vote.comment_id = comment.id)"})
selfVote: 1 | 0 | -1;
```

