@douglas-treadwell is right – and using getRawAndEntities is imo better fitted than https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/1822#issuecomment-573492291 as it allows you to omit the `@Column()` definition on the computed properties. Column decorators are problematic because TypeORM expects them in queries and the database schema, meaning if you `schema:sync`, it will actually try to create the column even though we don't want it. Here is how I did it, solving OP's usecase:

```ts
@Entity
export class Order extends BaseEntity {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id!: number;
  @ManyToOne(type => Person)
  person: Person;

  hasName?: boolean;
}

// ...

const raw_and_entities = createQueryBuilder("order")
  .leftJoinAndSelect("order.person", "person")
  .addSelect("person.name IS NOT NULL", "hasName")
  .getRawAndEntities()

for(const entity of raw_and_entities.entities) {
  const raw = raw_and_entities.raw.find(raw => raw.order_id === entity.id)
  entity.hasName = raw.hasName
}
const orders = raw_and_entities.entities
```

This requires some extra code of course and thus only makes sense when your computed column logic is a bit more complicated. In simple cases (like here, `IS NOT NULL`), you would probably rather just do the same thing in JS, preferrably via `@AfterLoad` like proposed by Offlein https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/issues/296#issuecomment-464416330

I encountered this issue recently, but came up with a workaround after spending an hour or so, and I thought I should share it with you all just in case.

My workaround is to use `@ViewEntity` with `@OneToOne`. say we have a `Book` entity
```
@Entity()
class Book {
  @PrimaryGeneratedColumn()
  id: number;
  
  @Column('timestamptz')
  publishedAt: Date;

  @OneToOne(() => BookComputed, bc => bc.book)
  computed: Book;
}
```
Then, let's say we want a computed column `publishedYear`. We can define a ViewEntity
```
@ViewEntity({
  expression: `SELECT id, EXTRACT(year from "publishedAt") "publishedYear" FROM book`
})
class BookComputed {
  @ViewColumn()
  id: number;
  
  @ViewColumn()
  publishedYear: number;

  @OneToOne(() => Book)
  @JoinColumn({name: 'id'})
  book: Book;
}
```
 
Then we can use it like
```
Book.findOneOrFail(id, {
  relations: ['computed']
});
```

